   "speed": 1.06
  }
 },
 "script": [
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "In nineteen seventy, the state of Oregon blew up a whale. On purpose. With half a ton of dynamite.",
   "fish_dir": "[a folksy storyteller, grinning, can't believe it himself]"
  },
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "And buddy, this ain't some tall tale. It's on film.",
   "fish_dir": "[leaning in, sly and warm]"
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Here's the deal. November, a little beach town called Florence. A forty-five foot sperm whale washes up dead. Eight tons. And it stinks to high heaven.",
   "fish_dir": "[easy, settling into the yarn]"
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Now, in Oregon, the beach counted as a highway. So the whale was the highway department's problem.",
   "fish_dir": "[dry, deadpan]"
  },
  {
   "beat": "plan",
   "text": "Their plan? Blow it into tiny pieces and let the seagulls clean up. What could go wrong?",
   "fish_dir": "[mock-serious, then deeply sarcastic on the last question]"
  },
  {
   "beat": "plan",
   "text": "One fella in the crowd, an old army explosives man, said that's way too much dynamite. Nobody listened.",
   "fish_dir": "[a little tense, ominous]"
  },
  {
   "beat": "boom",
   "text": "Twenty cases of dynamite. The countdown. And... KABOOM.",
   "fish_dir": "[building tension, slowing down, then shouting the last word]"
  },
  {
   "beat": "rain",
   "text": "Sand, smoke, and then it rained whale. Chunks of blubber came down on the crowd like the sky had a bellyache.",
   "fish_dir": "[awed, then laughing in disbelief]"
  },
  {
   "beat": "car",
   "text": "One chunk flattened a brand-new car. And whose car was it? The army man's. The way he told it, he bought it at a sale called Get a Whale of a Deal.",
   "fish_dir": "[gleeful, savoring the irony]"
  },
  {
   "beat": "gulls",
   "text": "And the seagulls? Scared off by the boom. Didn't touch a bite.",
   "fish_dir": "[amused, matter-of-fact]"
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "Most of the whale was still lying right there. So they buried it. Like they should've done in the first place.",
   "fish_dir": "[shaking his head, wry]"
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "Today, Florence has a park called Exploding Whale Memorial Park. That's real.",
   "fish_dir": "[proud, warm]"
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "So next time somebody says I've got a plan, ask how much dynamite. That's about it, folks. Follow for more strange history. See ya!",
   "fish_dir": "[warm, wrapping up with a wink, friendly sign-off]"
  }
 ],
 "science": [
  {
   "claim": "On 12 November 1970 the Oregon State Highway Division, which had jurisdiction over Oregon's beaches (legally public highways), removed a dead sperm whale about 45 ft long and about 8 tons from the beach at Florence by blasting it with about half a ton (20 cases) of dynamite; blubber fell on spectators up to a quarter mile away and one large piece crushed the roof of a new Oldsmobile belonging to Walter Umenhofer, who had warned it was too much dynamite and had bought the car during a 'Get a Whale of a Deal' promotion; seagulls were frightened away; most of the carcass remained and was buried. Footage by KATU (reporter Paul Linnman). In 2020 Florence named a park Exploding Whale Memorial Park.",
   "working": "",
   "source": "Wikipedia, Exploding whale; KATU archive; Oregon Department of Transportation; The Oregonian (2020)"
  }
 ],
